Cell[TextData[{
 "The animated version makes the shock/rarefaction asymmetry obvious. The \
initial high-density block has a sharp upstream edge (left) and a sharp \
downstream edge (right); the two evolve very differently:\n",
 "\[Bullet] Upstream, density rises from 20 to 120 veh/km and characteristic \
speed drops to negative values. Characteristics collide: a ",
 StyleBox["shockwave",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " forms and propagates backward \[LongDash] this is the classic signature of \
a jam viewed from the roadside.\n",
 "\[Bullet] Downstream, density drops from 120 to 20 veh/km and \
characteristics fan outward. No shock forms; instead the block melts into a \
smooth ",
 StyleBox["rarefaction wave",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " as cars accelerate out of the jam.\n",
 "Notice that the shock moves ",
 StyleBox["backward",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " even though every individual car moves forward \[LongDash] the very same \
phenomenon we'll see in the microscopic models later. The Greenshields \
fundamental diagram is a crude fit to empirical data, but LWR already \
captures the qualitative structure of a real jam."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9f5214ef-c05c-4dde-a650-733121d9776b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
lwrFrames = Table[CoreAnimations`LWRCorridorFrame[lwrRes, k],
  {k, 1, Length[lwrRes[\"t\"]], 2}];
ListAnimate[lwrFrames, AnimationRate -> 4]\
\>", "Input",
 CellTags->"runnable",ExpressionUUID->"51832feb-59ce-4e97-a5da-ca5430f092b3"],

