Cell["\<\
The backward-propagating jam waves appearing in the space-time diagram are \
the same bands we've already seen in the IDM animation (Section 4) and hinted \
at in LWR (Section 2). Three completely different mathematical frameworks \
\[LongDash] integer CA, nonlinear ODE system, hyperbolic PDE \[LongDash] \
converge on the same qualitative behaviour. This is a hallmark of \
universality: the stop-and-go wave is an emergent feature of the \
driver-follower relationship, robust to the choice of formalism.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"28c6ccd6-a9a2-4570-bb72-2064af6c41d7"],

Cell["\<\
Extending to two lanes with a symmetric lane-change rule (incentive: would \
have to brake in current lane; safety: sufficient gap ahead and behind in the \
target lane; target cell free) recovers the classic multi-lane redistribution:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2ca6a4a3-ea80-40d9-89c1-cd3b381d0e02"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
ns2Res = NagelSchreckenberg2Lane`SimulateNaSch2[
  \"T\" -> 200, \"nVehicles\" -> 150, \"roadLength\" -> 500];
