 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"cff6ff6d-587d-42a9-81fe-457e47b3116b"],

Cell["\<\
The Aberdeen Western Peripheral Route \[LongDash] opened 2018 to divert \
through-traffic around the city \[LongDash] is the big ring visible on the \
west/north perimeter, and you can watch it carry the lion's share of \
through-flow exactly as intended. This is the pay-off of the whole tour: once \
you have microscopic car-following, intersection control, and a real street \
graph, the city-scale pattern emerges without further tuning.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d5a372aa-303e-4b00-90f8-500b7177abdc"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["12. Conclusions", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"82e81d5c-34a0-4f2e-aa64-cbab56a695bc"],

Cell["\<\
A tour through five orders of magnitude of spatial scale \[LongDash] from a \
single vehicle's ODE up to a 11\[ThinSpace]925-node city-wide multi-agent \
simulation \[LongDash] and a few recurring themes:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"161df99a-cd3f-4b36-93bd-3a416d5e5661"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Phantom jams are universal.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " The same backward-propagating stop-and-go wave appears in a hyperbolic PDE \
(LWR shock), a 7-parameter nonlinear ODE system (IDM), a 3-parameter ODE \
system (Bando OVM), and an integer cellular automaton (NaSch). Four \
completely different formalisms, one qualitative answer. The wave itself is a \
genuine emergent feature of the follow-the-leader relationship, not an \
artefact of any specific model.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Stability is a bifurcation.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " For every model the transition from free flow to stop-and-go is a \
well-defined instability \[LongDash] a Hopf bifurcation in Bando OVM, a \
string-stability criterion in IDM, a phase transition in NaSch. Crossing the \
threshold doesn't just degrade the solution, it changes its qualitative \
character.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Selfish routing is suboptimal.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " The Wardrop equilibrium is a Nash equilibrium in the route-choice game, \
and like most Nash equilibria it's not socially optimal. Braess's paradox is \
the extreme version: adding capacity can make everyone worse off. This is why \
real-world network interventions (bus gates, road pricing, the AWPR) are \
political hard decisions even when the traffic physics is clear.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Signal timing is convex.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " The objective combining Webster's uniform delay and the M/D/1 waiting term \
is smooth and strictly convex \[LongDash] a tractable optimisation that \
routinely saves ~10% delay. Networked control (green waves) and adaptive \
systems like SCOOT/MOVA push that further but build on the same convex \
foundation.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Wolfram is well-suited to this.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " The entire port runs in ~40 seconds end-to-end on a laptop, with no \
external dependencies beyond ",
 StyleBox["FindMinimum", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["FindShortestPath", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["Graph", "Input"],
 ", and ",
 StyleBox["NDSolveValue", "Input"],
 ". Wolfram's built-in OSM and geocomputation support (",
 StyleBox["OpenStreetMapData", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["Import[\"*.osm\"]", "Input"],
 ") makes it plausible to skip the Python/NetworkX/OSMnx stack for \
research-scale work; the GitHub repo has both ports side-by-side."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c5e8520c-cd11-424b-9968-4e7c9f57a4c5"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["13. Implementation notes", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"1d518478-7387-4ceb-9999-e11aee6b4b93"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] Each model is a standalone .wl package with a ",
 StyleBox["Simulate<Name>[opts]", "Input"],
 " entry point returning an Association and a matching ",
 StyleBox["Plot<Name>[result]", "Input"],
 " renderer.\n",
 "\[Bullet] Car-following loops (IDM, Bando) use vectorised ",
 StyleBox["RotateLeft", "Input"],
 " to compute leader-follower gaps in one call.\n",
 "\[Bullet] Both network-assignment models use ",
 StyleBox["FindMinimum[{obj, constraints}, vars, Method -> \
\"InteriorPoint\"]", "Input"],
 " on the Beckmann integral.\n",
 "\[Bullet] Animations are plain ",
 StyleBox["ListAnimate", "Input"],
 "s over frame lists built by the functions in ",
 StyleBox["CoreAnimations.wl", "Input"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"09b5705d-1abd-4051-882e-72bb5cfc96e4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["14. References", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9e81146a-0140-4254-ac11-2e8389ce50f5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] M. J. Lighthill & G. B. Whitham (1955), \"On kinematic waves \
II\", ",
 StyleBox["Proc. Roy. Soc. A",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " 229:317.\n\[Bullet] ",
 "M. Treiber, A. Hennecke & D. Helbing (2000), \"Congested traffic states in \
empirical observations and microscopic simulations\", ",
 StyleBox["Phys. Rev. E",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " 62:1805.\n\[Bullet] ",
 "M. Treiber & A. Kesting (2013), ",
 StyleBox["Traffic Flow Dynamics",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", Springer.\n",
 "\[Bullet] K. Nagel & M. Schreckenberg (1992), ",
 StyleBox["J. Phys. I France",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " 2:2221.\n\[Bullet] M. Bando et al. (1995), ",
 StyleBox["Phys. Rev. E",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " 51:1035.\n\[Bullet] J. G. Wardrop (1952), ",
 StyleBox["Proc. ICE",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " 1(3):325.\n\[Bullet] Y. Sugiyama et al. (2008), ",
 StyleBox["New J. Phys.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " 10:033001. Video: ",
 ButtonBox["youtube.com/watch?v=Rryu85BtALM",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rryu85BtALM"], None}],
 ".\n\[Bullet] Source: ",
 ButtonBox["github.com/mthiel74/TrafficJAms",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://github.com/mthiel74/TrafficJAms"], None}],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a44cfe54-82e7-4027-b02f-63c762aae69a"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
